export const HANDBOOK = "# Terms (last updated 15 September 2026)\n\n## What this is\nFutureBox is a studio: you write songs with AI, sing on them yourself, clone your voice to read a podcast, and put a video to them. An account is free to open and you can use a real part of it without paying.\nYou need to be 18 or older to hold an account. If you are younger than that, a parent or guardian holds the account, agrees to this, and is responsible for what is made on it.\n\n## What you make is yours\nThe songs, recordings, videos and shows you make here belong to you. FutureBox does not take ownership of them and does not license them to anybody else.\nA song made with your free credits is a finished song. It is not marked, not degraded and not on loan; it is yours the same way a song made on a plan is. What a plan adds is more of them, the video engine, voice cloning and the rest — not the removal of something done to the free one.\nSelling what you make: where this actually stands. The music engine behind FutureBox runs on FutureBox's own paid plan, and that plan carries a commercial licence over what is generated on it, with no credit to the engine required. What is not settled is whether that licence reaches through to you. The engine's owner told us in writing on 9 September 2026 that a member of a platform selling what they made under the platform's account is “not explicitly covered” by the terms of the plan we are on.\nWhere that stands as of 15 September 2026: better than it did. The engine's owner publishes a separate agreement covering exactly this — a platform bundling their service into its own and passing it on to its members — and that agreement permits it from the plan FutureBox is now on, and forbids it on the plans below. We read that as covering you. We have asked them to confirm it in writing and they have not yet answered.\nSo we are still not going to put it flatly, because that would be us betting on our own reading of somebody else's contract and you would be the one carrying the bet. We are negotiating the written confirmation now. What we will tell you meanwhile is this: what you make is yours, nobody else takes ownership of it, we believe the commercial licence does reach you, and the day that is in writing this page says so plainly. If you intend to release something commercially before then, ask us and we will tell you exactly where it stands rather than guess.\nTwo further limits worth knowing, because they are real and not fine print:\n- Generated music is not guaranteed to be unique. An AI model can produce something close to what it produced for somebody else. FutureBox cannot promise originality and does not warrant that anything generated here is free of somebody else's rights.\n- Film, television, radio and studio games are outside that licence. Selling the song, streaming it, and putting it in your own videos are all covered. Placing generated music in a film, a television or radio broadcast, or a studio-published game is not, and needs a separate agreement with the engine's owner. If that is where a song of yours is going, tell us before you sign anything and we will point you at the right licence. YouTube, TikTok, Instagram and the platforms like them are not caught by this. They are named as being outside the restriction. It is the broadcast and cinema end that needs the separate licence, not your channel.\nBeyond those, satisfy yourself before you release anything commercially. That is your call to make and it is not one this app can make for you.\n\n## What you may not do here\nThese are the rules that get an account closed, and each exists because the harm is real rather than theoretical:\n- Clone a voice that is not yours. A voice identifies a person. Cloning one without them is impersonation whatever it was meant for. You confirm the voice is your own before a clone is made, and that confirmation is kept.\n- Train a sound on music you do not own. Same reason, same confirmation.\n- Upload recordings you have no right to.\n- Ask for a named person's voice or style. Prompts that name a real artist — “in the voice of”, “in the style of”, “sounds like” followed by somebody's name — are refused. Describe the sound instead: the tempo, the instruments, the era, the delivery. That is also how you get a result you are entitled to release.\n- Impersonate anybody, or present a generated recording as a real person having said or sung something, or make anything built to be taken as a real recording of a real event.\n- Use the app to make material that is illegal, that sexualises children, that incites violence against anybody, that harasses somebody, or that is a script for defrauding somebody — including anything read in the name of a bank, an insurer or an authority.\n- Resell access, share one account among several people, or run the app through your own service without an agreement.\nBreak these and the account is suspended or closed. Where the law requires it, the matter is reported.\n\n## How those rules are actually enforced\nThey are not only a document. Every prompt is checked on the server before it reaches an engine, by a fixed set of rules and then by a model that reads the sentence. A refused prompt costs you nothing: it is refused before any credit is taken and before anything is generated.\n- A refusal says what it refused and, where there is one, what would work instead. It is not a wall.\n- Refusals are recorded — the rule, where it happened, the time, and the first 200 characters of what was typed. Prompts that are allowed are not recorded this way.\n- Six refusals in thirty days stops the account generating. A prompt that merely strayed near a famous name does not count towards that; the serious categories do. Nothing is deleted, and you can write and say why it is wrong.\n- For video, for cloning a voice, and for training a sound, a request does not go ahead at all while the check cannot be run. Those three are refused rather than waved through.\nNo screen catches everything. This one will sometimes refuse something ordinary and will sometimes miss something it should have caught. When it refuses something it should not have, write and say so — that is how the rules get better rather than merely stricter.\n\n## The engines behind this, and what they require of you\nFutureBox does not make music or voices itself. It is built on other people's engines — ElevenLabs for music, voices, cloning and transcription, Anthropic for the copilot. When you generate something, what you typed or recorded goes to them, and their rules travel with it.\nFour things follow. They are not fine print: they are conditions of FutureBox being allowed to offer any of this, and they are written here because we are required to put them in front of you rather than merely comply with them ourselves.\n- Their rules bind you the way they bind us. ElevenLabs' terms of service and prohibited use policy govern what may be made here, and nothing on this page may be more permissive than they are. Where the list above and their policy differ, the stricter one is what is in force. Their policy is published at elevenlabs.io/use-policy and it changes from time to time; the version in force is the current one.\n- Nobody here is anybody's partner. FutureBox is not ElevenLabs' agent, partner, reseller or joint venturer, and neither are you. Do not describe yourself as working with them, endorsed by them, or acting for them.\n- ElevenLabs can enforce this agreement directly. They are a third-party beneficiary of the agreement between you and FutureBox. In plain terms: the parts of this document that protect them, they may act on themselves, without going through us.\n- They may process what you send them. By using the parts of FutureBox that reach an engine, you grant ElevenLabs, its affiliates and its subcontractors a non-exclusive right to process and use what you send, in order to provide and support the service. What that means in practice — including that a person may listen to a recording of you, and that the licence over a cloned voice outlives our copy of it — is set out in the privacy policy. Read it before you clone your voice, not after.\nNobody under 18's voice, at all. The voice engine's own rules prohibit sending it a recording of anybody under 18 — outright, with no parental-permission exception. So on an account a parent holds for somebody younger, the voice features are switched off: no cloning, no singing conversion, no reading in your own voice. The rest of the studio — writing, generating music, videos, the channel — works normally. This is not us being careful; it is a rule we are not allowed to waive.\n\n## If somebody has used your voice, your face or your name\nYou do not need an account here, and you do not need a lawyer, to have something taken down.\nTell us on the help page, with a link to what you have found and enough to show it is you. Anything that is a voice clone, a likeness or a recording of somebody who did not agree to it is taken down while it is looked at, rather than after. If it turns out to have been made from a cloned voice, that voice is deleted from the voice service as well as from here, and the account that made it is dealt with under the rules above.\nThe same page takes a copyright complaint, from a rights holder or from somebody acting for one.\n\n## Who may use this\nYou need to be 18 or older to hold an account and to pay for a plan. If you are younger than that, a parent or guardian holds the account and is responsible for what is made on it.\n\n## Credits, plans and what expires\nGenerating costs credits. A plan includes an allowance every month; a top-up pack is bought separately.\n- A month's allowance is for that month. It does not roll over, and it is granted on your first visit of the month rather than by a clock.\n- Bought credits do not expire while your account exists, and are used before the month's allowance.\n- A failed generation is refunded. Credits are taken before the work is asked for and given back if it does not happen. If you believe something was charged and not delivered, write and it will be put right.\n- A top-up pack costs more per credit than a plan does. That is stated where the packs are shown; the plan is the cheaper way to get credits every month.\n\n## Paying, and stopping\nPlans are monthly and renew until you cancel. Payments are taken by Paystack; no card details pass through FutureBox.\nCancel any time, from inside the app. A month you have already paid for runs to its end — it is not refunded and it is not cut short. Nothing more is charged after that.\nIf a price changes, anybody on a plan is told by email before it applies to them, and can cancel first.\nSouth African consumer law gives you rights that this document cannot take away, including in respect of goods and services that are not as described. Nothing here limits them.\n\n## Deleting your account\nYou can delete your account from inside the app. It removes your songs, videos and episodes and every file behind them, any cloned voice — from the voice service as well as from here — any trained sound, your credits, your collaborations and your profile.\nIt cannot be undone. There is no grace period on purpose: keeping recordings of your voice for a month after you asked us to stop would be the opposite of what you asked for. If you are on a plan it is cancelled first, and if that cancellation fails nothing is deleted.\nWhat deletion does not reach. The clone is deleted, and that is real. What outlives it is a licence the voice engine already holds over the recordings you gave it: by their terms it is perpetual and cannot be withdrawn, and their own retention limit for data generated about a voice is three years after your last contact with them, not the moment you press the button. We can delete what is ours and we can tell them to delete the model, and we do both. We cannot unwind a licence somebody else already has, and we are not going to imply otherwise on this page. If that is not acceptable to you, the answer is to not clone your voice — everything else here works without it.\n\n## What FutureBox does not promise\nThe app is provided as it is. It is not promised to be available without interruption, and a generation is not promised to be good, unique, or fit for any particular purpose.\nParts of it depend on services run by other people — ElevenLabs for music and voices, Anthropic for the copilot, Supabase, Vercel, Paystack. If one of them is down or changes what it offers, the part of FutureBox that uses it is affected, and that is outside anyone's control here.\nWhere the law allows a limit, FutureBox's liability to you is limited to what you have paid it in the twelve months before whatever went wrong. Nothing here limits liability for death, personal injury, fraud, or anything else the law does not permit to be limited.\n\n## Suspending an account\nAn account can be suspended or closed for breaking the rules above, for using the app in a way that costs money to serve and was obviously not intended, or where the law requires it. Where it is fair to do so you are told why and given a chance to answer.\n\n## Changes to these terms\nIf these change in a way that affects you, the date at the top changes and anybody with an account is told by email before it takes effect. Carrying on using the app after that is how you accept them; if you would rather not, delete the account.\n\n## Law, and getting hold of us\nSouth African law applies, and the courts of South Africa have jurisdiction.\nThis document is written in English, and the English version is the one that governs. Parts of the app are shown in Afrikaans; where a translation and this document disagree, this document is what was agreed to.\nAsk about anything in this document on the help page. See also the privacy policy, which covers what is kept and who sees it.\n\n# Privacy policy (last updated 24 September 2026)\n\n## What is kept, and why\nYour account. An email address, so you can sign back in and so a payment can be tied to you. If you sign in with Google we receive the email address and name on that account and nothing else — no contacts, no calendar, no drive.\nYour songs. The title, genre, tempo, key, lyrics and style words you wrote, and the audio itself. Audio lives in a private bucket; the rules on it allow one account to reach one folder, and that folder is yours.\nRecordings of your voice. If you clone your voice, the minute you record is sent to ElevenLabs and the clone is kept on their account. We store a row saying that voice is yours and the moment you confirmed, in words, that it is your own voice. That confirmation is kept because consent that cannot be produced afterwards is not consent.\nSongs you train a sound on. Sent to ElevenLabs for training, with a record of whether they came from your channel or you brought them, and your confirmation that the music is yours.\nWhat you have made and spent. Songs generated, credits granted and spent, purchases. This is how an allowance is counted; without it the free tier would be a suggestion.\nYour public profile, if you make one. A name, an @handle, a short description and links you choose to add. This is meant to be seen — it is how somebody finds you to work with. Songs appear on the collaboration radar only for songs you switch on, one at a time, and switching one off removes it.\nCollaboration. A request between you and one other person, and the messages afterwards. Nobody else can read them, and neither of you can read them before you have both agreed — that is enforced in the database, not by a screen.\nWhat kind of thing you make. A count. When you finish a song the genre is added to a tally against your account — “dubstep, eleven” — and when you open a room, the room is. That is the whole of it: a label, how many times, and when it last happened. It is what lets the welcome screen and the copilot suggest the kind of thing you actually make instead of asking every time, and it is why that works on your phone as well as your laptop.What is deliberately not kept is the times themselves. There is no row per song and no row per visit, so no record exists of when you work, how long for, or in what order — the app cannot reconstruct that because it was never written down. You can see the whole tally on your account screen and clear it there, which stops the suggestions and deletes the counts.\nPhotographs of people, if you add a cast. The video desk lets you keep a small cast — a picture and a name for each person a presenter clip can be made of. The picture is kept in the same private bucket as your audio, and when you make a clip it is sent to ElevenLabs to be animated. A photograph of somebody is information about them whether or not they hold the account, which is why the app asks you to confirm the face is yours or that you have their permission before a clip is made, and why that confirmation is kept. Take a cast member out and the picture goes with them.\nWho you work together with. When two people accept a collaboration and open a shared room, a row records the pair and which room it is. If either of you chooses to share a social link so the two of you can talk elsewhere, that link is kept against the pair and shown to the other person — only to them, and only after you both agreed.\nThe live room. What you post there, what you mark as liked, and a note that you are currently watching. If you report somebody’s link, a row records that you reported it — that is how the same link being reported twice takes it down, and it means a report is not anonymous to us even though it is to the person reported.\nBids on album art. The once-off fee that makes you a bidder on a piece, every bid you place, and what you won. An auction cannot work without a record of who bid what, and the artist can see the bids on their own piece.\nAnything you queue to post. The marketing desk can hold a caption and a date so it can remind you when the time comes. What is kept is what you wrote and which platform it is for. It reminds rather than posts — nothing is sent anywhere on your behalf from that queue.\nA word that came out wrong. If you tell us an Afrikaans word was said badly, what is kept is the word, how you say it should sound, and which room you were in. It is how the pronunciation list gets better, and it is read by a person before anything changes.\nCompetitions, if you enter one. Your entry, and whether it won.\nPrompts that were refused. When the safety check refuses something you asked for, what is written down is the rule that refused it, where in the app it happened, the time, and the first 200 characters of what you typed. Prompts that are allowed are not written down this way, and none of this is read by anybody unless a refusal has to be looked into. It is kept because a platform that says it enforces its rules and cannot show a single instance of doing so is making a claim rather than a statement.\n\n## What is never kept\nYour card. Payments go to Paystack, on their page. No card number, expiry or CVV passes through this app or is stored by it. What comes back is a reference and a customer code.\nYour address, as an address. The IP a request arrives from is hashed with a secret before anything is written down, and only the hash is stored. It is used for two things: noticing that a hundred free accounts came from one place, and noticing that a suspended account has come back from the same machine. The address itself is not recoverable from what is kept.\n\n## Who else sees it\nOnly what each one needs to do its job:\n- Supabase — the database, the audio, and sign-in.\n- Vercel — hosting. Requests pass through it.\n- ElevenLabs — music, voices and trained sounds. Lyrics, style words, recordings of your voice and songs you train on are sent there.\n- Anthropic — the copilot and the writing help. What you type into those goes there.\n- Paystack — payments. Your email, so a receipt reaches you.\n- Kits.AI — singing conversion. When you put a take through “sing it in another voice”, the recording of you singing is sent to them. It is a recording of your voice, and everything this page says below about what happens to one applies here too.\n- Music.ai — reading a song. When you ask for the chords, the key, the tempo or where the sections are, the audio is sent to them to be listened to.\n- Resend — email. Your address and the letter itself, whenever anything is sent to you: an invitation, a password reset, a receipt.\n- Kling — video, where a clip is generated on their engine. What you typed to describe the clip goes there.\n- Google — only if you choose to sign in with it.\nKits.AI, Music.ai, Resend and Kling were added to this list on 24 September 2026, in a legal audit before handing the app over to be rated. They had been receiving what is described above for some time; the list had six names on it and should have had ten. Nothing about what is sent changed — what changed is that this page now says so.\nNothing is sold, and nothing is handed to an advertiser. There is no advertising on FutureBox to hand it to.\n\n## What happens to a recording once it leaves here\nThe voice engine is a company in its own right with its own terms, and once a recording of you reaches it, what happens next is governed by those and not only by this page. Four things are worth knowing before you clone your voice, and none of them is obvious from the outside.\n- A person may listen to it. The voice engine reserves the right to moderate everything sent to it and everything it produces, and to pass that material to third parties who help them do it. This is how a platform stops itself being used to fake somebody's voice, and it is the reason our own safety check is not the only one. But it does mean a recording of you is not machine-only.\n- It leaves South Africa. They host in the United States, the Netherlands and Singapore, and everything is stored in the United States regardless of where it was recorded. POPIA allows that where the receiving party is bound to comparable protection; they are, under the European standard contractual clauses in the data-processing agreement that applies to our account.\n- Deleting the clone does not delete their licence. When you delete a cloned voice, the model is deleted from their side as well as ours — that part is real and this app does it. What we cannot undo is the licence they already hold over the recordings you gave them, which by their own terms is perpetual, and their retention limit for data generated about a voice is three years from your last contact with them. Everything else on this page about deletion is accurate; this is the edge of it.\n- Your voice is treated here as special personal information. A voice identifies a person the way a fingerprint does, and POPIA puts that in a stricter category than an email address. That is why cloning asks you to confirm in words that the voice is your own, and why that confirmation is stored with the moment you gave it. It is also why the voice features are switched off entirely for anybody under 18 — the engine's own rules prohibit sending it a child's voice, with no parental-permission exception.\nIf that is more than you want, you do not have to clone anything. Writing songs, generating music, making videos and running a channel all work without a recording of your voice ever leaving this app.\nAnd it is not only the voice engine any more. Everything above is about ElevenLabs, where a cloned voice goes. Singing conversion — “sing it in another voice” — sends your recording to a different company, Kits.AI, in the United States.\nWe are not going to pretend the same protections cover both. With ElevenLabs there is a signed data-processing agreement, a stated retention limit and standard contractual clauses that POPIA accepts as the basis for the recording leaving the country. With Kits.AI, as of 24 September 2026, we do not have those on file. Their published terms govern what they do with it, we have asked them for an agreement, and until there is one this page is going to keep saying this rather than leaving you to assume.\nWhat that means for you, plainly: if you want your voice to go no further than the engine we have an agreement with, use the voice features and not the singing conversion. Everything else in the booth — recording, takes, the timeline, mixing — happens on your own device and sends your voice nowhere at all.\nThe full list of companies the voice engine uses behind the scenes is published by them at compliance.elevenlabs.io, and they are required to give us thirty days' notice before adding one. If one of them is ever the reason something here has to change, the date at the top of this page changes with it.\n\n## How long\nYour songs, voices and profile stay until you delete them or delete your account. Counting rows — what was generated, what credits moved — are kept while the account exists, because an allowance that forgets is not an allowance.\nThe tally of what kind of thing you make stays until you clear it on your account screen, or until the account goes.\nThe live visitor count keeps a random id your browser made up and a timestamp, for two minutes. It is not tied to your account and it disappears on its own.\n\n## Deleting it\nYou can clear the tally of what you make on your account screen, on its own, without deleting anything else — wanting the suggestions to stop is not the same as wanting the account gone, and only offering the second is not offering a choice.\nYou can delete any song, any cloned voice and any trained sound from inside the app at any time. Deleting a voice or a sound removes it from ElevenLabs as well as from here — removing only our row would be hiding it, not deleting it.\nTo delete your whole account and everything in it, there is a button at the bottom of your channel. It asks you to type your email address first, and then it is immediate: the songs, the videos, the episodes and every file behind them, any cloned voice — from ElevenLabs as well as from here — any trained sound, your credits, your collaborations and the account itself. If you are on a plan it is cancelled first, and if that cancellation fails nothing is deleted, because being charged for an account you no longer have is worse than still having it.\nOne thing does not go with it. A record of a refused prompt stays, with the account detached from it — the rule, the time and a hashed address, and no name, no email and no account. A platform that forgets every refusal the moment somebody signs up again has no memory at all. Everything that identifies you is gone.\nIf you would rather it were done for you, ask on the help page from the address you signed up with, so it can be matched to your account.\n\n## What you can ask for\nA copy of what is held about you, a correction to anything wrong in it, or its deletion. Ask on the help page from the address you signed up with, so it can be matched to your account. Under South Africa's POPIA you may also complain to the Information Regulator if you are not satisfied with the answer. Who FutureBox is, in the words the law asks for, is on the supplier page.\n\n## Children\nAn account is held by somebody 18 or older. FutureBox is not built for children, and an account is not knowingly created for anybody under 18 — where a younger person uses it, a parent or guardian holds the account and agrees to this on their behalf. If you believe an account has been created for a child without that, say so on the help page and it will be removed.\nOne rule has no exception. No recording of anybody under 18 may be sent to the voice engine — not with a parent's permission, not on a parent's account. So where an account is held by a parent for a younger person, the voice features are off: no cloning, no singing conversion, no reading in your own voice. Everything else works.\n\n## If something goes wrong with it\nIf personal information held here is ever accessed by somebody who should not have it, you will be told: what happened, what of yours was involved, and what to do about it. So will South Africa's Information Regulator. That is what POPIA requires and it is not conditional on the breach being our fault — if it happens at one of the suppliers named above and they tell us, we tell you.\nYou do not have to wait to be told. If you think something is wrong with your account, say so on the help page and it will be looked at rather than triaged.\n\n## Changes\nIf this changes in a way that affects what is collected or who sees it, the date at the top changes and anybody with an account is told by email before it takes effect.";
